@using HTMX.Application.Web.Security
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>@ViewData["Title"] · HTMLX Pages</title>
    @* The one stylesheet, which Tailwind CSS compiles from Styles/app.css as the application builds. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/app.css" />
    @*
        The brand's favicon, copied from its skill. A browser that draws an SVG icon takes favicon.svg, sharp at any
        size; one that does not takes favicon.ico. The touch icon is for a page saved to a phone's home screen.
        favicon.ico is also at the root, where a browser asks for it when a page names no icon.
    *@
    <link rel="icon" href="~/favicon.ico" />
    <link rel="icon" href="~/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="~/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    @*
        HTMX 4.0.1, the one version this application is built and tested with; README.md records it. The scripts sit in
        the head because HTMX 4 answers the back button by swapping a fresh page body in: a script in the body would
        run again, and load a second HTMX. Each carries this response's nonce, which its Content Security Policy names,
        so the browser runs these scripts and no other. The second gives HTMX the Trusted Types policy that the
        Content Security Policy asks for, before HTMX swaps anything in.
    *@
    @{
        string nonce = ContentSecurityPolicy.Nonce(Context);
    }
    <script src="~/js/htmx.min.js" defer nonce="@nonce"></script>
    <script src="~/js/htmx-trusted-types.js" defer nonce="@nonce"></script>
    <script src="~/js/app.js" defer nonce="@nonce"></script>
</head>
@*
    Every link and form in the application loads over HTMX: a link to another page of it fetches that page and swaps
    its body into this one, keeps the address bar and Back in step, and takes the new page's title. HTMX leaves alone a
    link to another site, one that opens a new tab, one to a part of this page (#main) and a form that says
    method="dialog". A form that posts says hx-post, which HTMX sets up before boosting, so it keeps its own target. A
    page that HTMX loaded puts the keyboard on its heading, as each page model says, since HTMX moves it nowhere.
    Without HTMX every link and form works as it always did.
*@
<body hx-boost:inherited="true">
    @* The skip link waits above the window until the keyboard reaches it. *@
    <a class="absolute -top-[100px] left-2 z-100 rounded-sm bg-surface px-3 py-2 focus:top-2" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

    @*
        The application shell: the header across the top, the side nav, and the page's main content beside it. The nav
        is full from 900px and an icon rail below that; the menu button switches it, and the application's script
        records the choice in data-nav, which the rail: variant reads. --nav holds the nav's width for the grid.
    *@
    <div id="app-shell" class="grid min-h-screen grid-cols-[var(--nav)_minmax(0,1fr)] grid-rows-[var(--header-height)_1fr] [--nav:var(--nav-width)] rail:[--nav:var(--nav-rail-width)]">
        <header class="sticky top-0 z-10 col-span-2 flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-line bg-surface px-2">
            <button button="icon" type="button" data-action="toggle-nav" aria-label="Main menu" aria-controls="app-nav">
                <icon name="menu" />
            </button>
            <a class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-strong text-fg hover:no-underline" asp-page="/Index">
                <svg class="size-7 flex-none" aria-hidden="true"><use href="/icons.svg#logo"></use></svg>
                HTMLX Pages
            </a>
            <div class="ml-auto flex items-center gap-2">
                <span class="inline-grid size-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-subtle text-xs font-strong uppercase text-brand-text" role="img" aria-label="Signed in as Demo User">DU</span>
            </div>
        </header>

        @*
            Each page names its section in ViewData, and the section's link is marked as the current page. The foot of
            the nav holds the one link that leaves the application, to its source on GitHub, which opens a new tab. The
            four links look alike, so their classes are written once, here. In the rail a link's label leaves the screen
            but stays for a screen reader, and its title gives a tooltip.
        *@
        @{
            string navLink = "group flex h-10 items-center gap-3 whitespace-nowrap px-[14px] text-fg hover:bg-surface-hover hover:no-underline aria-[current=page]:bg-accent-subtle aria-[current=page]:font-strong aria-[current=page]:text-accent aria-[current=page]:shadow-[inset_3px_0_0_var(--accent)]";
            string navIcon = "text-icon group-aria-[current=page]:text-accent";
        }
        <nav id="app-nav" class="sticky top-header flex h-[calc(100vh-var(--header-height))] flex-col gap-0.5 overflow-x-hidden overflow-y-auto border-r border-line bg-surface py-2" aria-label="Main">
            <a class="@navLink" asp-page="/Organizations/Index" title="Organizations"
               aria-current="@(ViewData["Section"] as string == "Organizations" ? "page" : null)">
                <icon name="building" class="@navIcon" /><span class="rail:sr-only">Organizations</span>
            </a>
            <a class="@navLink" asp-page="/Users/Index" title="Users"
               aria-current="@(ViewData["Section"] as string == "Users" ? "page" : null)">
                <icon name="users" class="@navIcon" /><span class="rail:sr-only">Users</span>
            </a>
            <a class="@navLink" asp-page="/Components/Index" title="Components"
               aria-current="@(ViewData["Section"] as string == "Components" ? "page" : null)">
                <icon name="palette" class="@navIcon" /><span class="rail:sr-only">Components</span>
            </a>
            <div class="mt-auto flex flex-col gap-0.5 border-t border-line pt-2">
                <a class="@navLink" href="https://github.com/mattonweb/Razor-Pages-With-HTMX" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                   title="GitHub (opens in a new tab)">
                    <icon name="external" class="@navIcon" /><span class="rail:sr-only">GitHub</span><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
                </a>
            </div>
        </nav>

        <main id="main" class="px-8 pt-6 pb-8 max-md:p-4">
            @RenderBody()
        </main>
    </div>

    @*
        The one region toasts appear in, as the brand asks. It is empty unless a page puts a toast in its Toasts section;
        after an action HTMX sent, the response replaces what it holds. Being a live region from the start, it has
        screen readers announce each toast as it arrives.
    *@
    <div id="toasts" class="pointer-events-none fixed bottom-6 left-1/2 z-50 grid w-max max-w-[calc(100vw-2*var(--space-4))] -translate-x-1/2 justify-items-center gap-2" role="status" aria-live="polite">@await RenderSectionAsync("Toasts", required: false)</div>

    @*
        What the application's script puts in the toast region when a request HTMX sent fails, in place of swapping
        anything: an answer the page did not plan for, no answer before HTMX gives up waiting, or none at all. The
        toasts are written here, in the brand's markup, so that the script holds no words of its own. A template's
        content is not part of the page until the script copies it out, and without script it never is.
    *@
    @{
        Toast unplannedAnswer = new Toast
        {
            Message = "Something went wrong, so that may not have been done. Reload the page, then try again.",
        };
        Toast timedOut = new Toast
        {
            Message = "The server took too long to answer, so that may not have been done. "
                + "Reload the page, then try again.",
        };
        Toast connectionLost = new Toast
        {
            Message = "The page couldn't reach the server. Check your connection, then try again.",
        };
    }
    <template id="request-failed-answer"><partial name="_Toast" model="unplannedAnswer" /></template>
    <template id="request-failed-timeout"><partial name="_Toast" model="timedOut" /></template>
    <template id="request-failed-connection"><partial name="_Toast" model="connectionLost" /></template>
    @await RenderSectionAsync("Scripts", required: false)
</body>
</html>
